1
00:00:00,055 --> 00:00:02,350
Here's an experiment you
can do with your lunch.

2
00:00:02,635 --> 00:00:03,900
Take a plain cracker,

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,220
one without added sugar,

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,525
and keep chewing.

5
00:00:06,545 --> 00:00:07,600
Don't swallow.

6
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,040
Give it a minute or so…

7
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,895
and it starts to
taste a little sweet.

8
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:12,640
But you didn't add any sugar.

9
00:00:12,730 --> 00:00:15,035
So where's that
sweetness coming from?

10
00:00:15,055 --> 00:00:17,010
A molecule in your saliva,

11
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:20,305
doing in a minute what would otherwise
take a million years or more.

12
00:00:23,216 --> 00:00:25,321
Crackers are packed with starch.

13
00:00:25,341 --> 00:00:27,726
Zoom right in, and starch is a chain:

14
00:00:27,871 --> 00:00:29,386
hundreds, even thousands,

15
00:00:29,406 --> 00:00:30,876
of glucose molecules,

16
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:32,231
linked end to end.

17
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:33,942
Glucose is a sugar,

18
00:00:33,962 --> 00:00:35,736
but locked up in a chain like this,

19
00:00:35,767 --> 00:00:37,096
it doesn't taste sweet.

20
00:00:37,282 --> 00:00:38,961
And those links are tough.

21
00:00:38,981 --> 00:00:41,306
Left alone in water
at room temperature,

22
00:00:41,386 --> 00:00:42,892
a single link would last,

23
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:44,831
on average, millions of years.

24
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:46,240
Enter amylase:

25
00:00:46,335 --> 00:00:48,810
an enzyme made in
your salivary glands.

26
00:00:49,030 --> 00:00:50,545
It's a carbohydrase:

27
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:52,910
it snips starch into smaller sugars,

28
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:56,775
like maltose, which is just two
glucose units joined together.

29
00:00:56,795 --> 00:00:58,675
And maltose does taste sweet.

30
00:00:58,955 --> 00:01:00,320
That's what an enzyme is:

31
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:02,105
a biological catalyst.

32
00:01:02,295 --> 00:01:05,575
It speeds up a reaction without
being used up or changed.

33
00:01:05,595 --> 00:01:07,630
And nearly every
reaction in your body,

34
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,520
building things up or
breaking them down,

35
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:10,500
has one.

36
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:15,165
Without enzymes, your metabolism would
be far too slow to keep you alive.

37
00:01:15,185 --> 00:01:17,365
Nearly all enzymes are proteins,

38
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:21,225
long chains of amino acids
folded into a precise shape.

39
00:01:21,245 --> 00:01:22,810
And that shape is everything.

40
00:01:23,009 --> 00:01:26,109
Every enzyme has a small
pocket called the active site.

41
00:01:26,359 --> 00:01:27,759
The molecule it works on,

42
00:01:27,779 --> 00:01:29,889
the substrate, fits inside,

43
00:01:29,949 --> 00:01:32,679
forming an enzyme–substrate complex.

44
00:01:32,699 --> 00:01:35,899
Held there, a bond becomes
much easier to break.

45
00:01:35,919 --> 00:01:37,889
Snap. The products leave,

46
00:01:37,909 --> 00:01:40,614
and the active site is
free for the next one.

47
00:01:40,634 --> 00:01:43,729
The active site's shape is
complementary to the substrate:

48
00:01:43,749 --> 00:01:44,774
not the same shape,

49
00:01:44,794 --> 00:01:46,019
but a perfect fit.

50
00:01:46,279 --> 00:01:47,744
The enzyme is the lock;

51
00:01:47,764 --> 00:01:49,539
the substrate is the key.

52
00:01:49,559 --> 00:01:51,509
That's why enzymes are specific:

53
00:01:51,594 --> 00:01:52,704
each one only fits,

54
00:01:52,724 --> 00:01:55,044
and works on, its own substrate.

55
00:01:55,064 --> 00:01:57,379
Amylase can't even
break down cellulose,

56
00:01:57,399 --> 00:02:00,089
in paper, though that's
a chain of glucose too.

57
00:02:00,339 --> 00:02:02,029
Its links are joined differently,

58
00:02:02,049 --> 00:02:03,319
so it just doesn't fit.

59
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:05,460
Lock and key is a brilliant model,

60
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:07,065
but it is a simplification.

61
00:02:07,305 --> 00:02:09,180
Real enzymes aren't rigid.

62
00:02:09,465 --> 00:02:10,780
As the substrate binds,

63
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,260
the active site moulds
itself around it.

64
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,950
That more accurate picture
is called induced fit.

65
00:02:16,245 --> 00:02:19,105
For GCSE and IGCSE,

66
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:20,780
you only need lock and key;

67
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:23,410
induced fit is an A-level idea.

68
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:24,430
Just remember:

69
00:02:24,515 --> 00:02:26,235
it's a model, not a photograph.

70
00:02:26,632 --> 00:02:28,382
Which brings us to myth number one:

71
00:02:28,552 --> 00:02:30,261
that enzymes get used up.

72
00:02:30,281 --> 00:02:31,327
They don't.

73
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:32,682
After each reaction,

74
00:02:32,702 --> 00:02:34,526
the enzyme is unchanged,

75
00:02:34,552 --> 00:02:35,542
ready to go again,

76
00:02:35,562 --> 00:02:36,577
and again.

77
00:02:36,597 --> 00:02:38,997
And some are astonishingly fast.

78
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:40,692
One molecule of catalase,

79
00:02:40,712 --> 00:02:42,427
found in almost all your cells,

80
00:02:42,507 --> 00:02:46,506
can break down hundreds of thousands
of molecules of hydrogen peroxide

81
00:02:46,526 --> 00:02:47,487
every second.

82
00:02:47,662 --> 00:02:50,102
Your cells do replace old enzymes,

83
00:02:50,122 --> 00:02:53,712
eventually. But the reaction
itself never uses them up.

84
00:02:54,101 --> 00:02:55,376
Now, temperature.

85
00:02:55,556 --> 00:02:57,896
Molecules are always jiggling around.

86
00:02:58,121 --> 00:03:00,491
Heat them up, and they
gain kinetic energy:

87
00:03:00,566 --> 00:03:02,041
they move faster.

88
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:05,171
Enzymes and substrates bump
into each other more often,

89
00:03:05,191 --> 00:03:06,486
and with more energy.

90
00:03:06,626 --> 00:03:08,261
More successful collisions,

91
00:03:08,281 --> 00:03:10,541
where the substrate
lands in the active site,

92
00:03:10,626 --> 00:03:13,256
mean more enzyme–substrate complexes:

93
00:03:13,476 --> 00:03:15,521
a faster rate of reaction.

94
00:03:15,541 --> 00:03:18,061
Up to a point, warmer means faster.

95
00:03:18,376 --> 00:03:21,571
The temperature where the rate
peaks is called the optimum.

96
00:03:21,591 --> 00:03:23,286
For many enzymes in your body,

97
00:03:23,306 --> 00:03:25,166
it's around 37 degrees:

98
00:03:25,311 --> 00:03:26,321
body temperature.

99
00:03:26,751 --> 00:03:28,486
So what if we keep
turning up the heat?

100
00:03:28,566 --> 00:03:29,591
Fifty degrees.

101
00:03:29,706 --> 00:03:31,351
Sixty. Seventy.

102
00:03:31,411 --> 00:03:33,486
Does it just keep getting faster?

103
00:03:33,506 --> 00:03:36,646
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

104
00:03:39,611 --> 00:03:41,216
Here's what actually happens.

105
00:03:41,391 --> 00:03:43,586
The rate crashes, and
it doesn't recover.

106
00:03:43,776 --> 00:03:47,391
An enzyme's shape is held
together by lots of weak bonds.

107
00:03:47,411 --> 00:03:51,821
Too much heat, and it vibrates so
violently that those bonds break.

108
00:03:51,841 --> 00:03:53,206
The chain unravels,

109
00:03:53,226 --> 00:03:55,141
the active site loses its shape,

110
00:03:55,291 --> 00:03:57,341
and the substrate no longer fits.

111
00:03:57,576 --> 00:03:59,596
The enzyme has been denatured.

112
00:03:59,616 --> 00:04:03,511
It's the same kind of change that
turns egg white solid in a hot pan,

113
00:04:03,741 --> 00:04:05,081
and, like a cooked egg,

114
00:04:05,211 --> 00:04:07,426
cooling it down usually
doesn't undo it.

115
00:04:07,556 --> 00:04:09,076
Cold is different.

116
00:04:09,096 --> 00:04:11,351
Molecules have less kinetic energy,

117
00:04:11,371 --> 00:04:12,821
so there are fewer collisions,

118
00:04:12,841 --> 00:04:14,956
but the enzyme isn't denatured.

119
00:04:14,976 --> 00:04:16,821
Warm it up, and it recovers.

120
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:18,505
And that's myth number two:

121
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:20,475
that heat "kills" enzymes.

122
00:04:20,495 --> 00:04:22,360
Enzymes were never alive.

123
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:24,094
An enzyme is a molecule,

124
00:04:24,114 --> 00:04:25,124
a very clever one,

125
00:04:25,144 --> 00:04:26,250
but it can't die.

126
00:04:26,524 --> 00:04:27,654
It's denatured:

127
00:04:27,674 --> 00:04:29,464
its active site changes shape,

128
00:04:29,484 --> 00:04:31,369
so the substrate no longer fits,

129
00:04:31,389 --> 00:04:34,480
and no enzyme–substrate
complex can form.

130
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,289
In an exam, that's the
answer that gets the marks.

131
00:04:37,562 --> 00:04:39,027
pH can do this too.

132
00:04:39,248 --> 00:04:40,862
Too acidic, or too alkaline,

133
00:04:40,882 --> 00:04:44,143
and the bonds holding the active
site in shape are disrupted.

134
00:04:44,163 --> 00:04:46,648
Go far enough, and the
enzyme is denatured.

135
00:04:46,668 --> 00:04:49,207
So every enzyme has an optimum pH.

136
00:04:49,452 --> 00:04:50,622
Amylase, in your mouth,

137
00:04:50,642 --> 00:04:52,063
works best close to neutral,

138
00:04:52,083 --> 00:04:53,638
around pH 7.

139
00:04:53,658 --> 00:04:55,422
Your stomach is strongly acidic,

140
00:04:55,442 --> 00:04:56,987
often around pH 2,

141
00:04:57,007 --> 00:04:59,853
and pepsin, a protease that
digests protein there,

142
00:04:59,873 --> 00:05:01,718
works best right around that.

143
00:05:01,738 --> 00:05:02,723
Different enzymes,

144
00:05:02,743 --> 00:05:04,217
tuned to different places.

145
00:05:04,575 --> 00:05:06,080
So, back to that cracker.

146
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:08,800
As you chew, amylase
meets the starch.

147
00:05:09,065 --> 00:05:10,825
The chains fit its active site,

148
00:05:10,890 --> 00:05:12,500
and it snips them into maltose,

149
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,740
over and over, without being used up.

150
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:18,180
Your mouth is about 37
degrees and close to neutral:

151
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,355
just right.

152
00:05:19,375 --> 00:05:22,740
Links that would last a million
years or more on their own are broken

153
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:23,890
while you chew.

154
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:26,020
That sweetness is enzymes at work,

155
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:27,615
in real time.

156
00:05:27,635 --> 00:05:29,570
And that's how enzymes actually work.
